<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/intermediate/a-history-of-architectural-styles">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Architectural history is the record of <strong>what each era believed a building should
    be</strong> — permanence, heaven, industry, function, irony, flow. Styles are not fashion
    cycles but worldviews in stone (the historiography lesson\'s framing, built).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="classical grammar, the vertical race, the industrial rupture, the modern cascade" widget="equationStep" config={styleCfg} />

  <h2>Reading buildings historically</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Materials write the chapters:</strong> load-bearing stone → arches; iron frames → tall; steel + curtain wall → glass towers; concrete → spans and sculptural form; engineered timber → the current sustainable turn (the materials ladder IS this story\'s spine).</li>
    <li><strong>Colonial and vernacular architectures enter the canon:</strong> Indo-Saracenic, Ottoman, Mughal (the Taj as symmetry and craft), Andalusian — and vernacular wisdom (courtyards, wind towers, thick walls — the passive lesson\'s pre-industrial science) finally studied as design intelligence, not folk art.</li>
    <li><strong>Why styles ended with modernism:</strong> the moderns claimed one universal style instead of period styles — then postmodernism argued there is no single truth (the philosophy lesson\'s modernism/postmodernism debate, with buildings as the exhibits).</li>
    <li><strong>Preservation as a field:</strong> what do we keep, and how honestly (restore or stabilise)? Venice Charter principles — history\'s values made explicit in conservation decisions (the archaeology lesson\'s ethics, extended to standing buildings).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Watkin, A History of Western Architecture; Frampton, Modern Architecture: A Critical History; the art-history ladder of this atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
